# `Stream<T>`

`Stream<T>` is the abstract type of values produced in a stable order,
`x0, x1, x2, …`, whose production need not end. A stream has no size, no
membership and no global order, because each of those needs the end. The
design is [HQL-0006](../../../proposals/HQL-0006/README.md); the declaration
is `std/core/mod.hql`, and the operations over it are `std/collections/mod.hql`'s.

```hql
abstract type Iterable<T>
abstract type Stream<T> : Iterable<T>
abstract type Collection<T> : Iterable<T>
abstract type Seq<T> : {Collection<T>, Stream<T>}
```

`Seq<T>` is where the two families meet: a sequence is a finite collection
and a stream that ends, so a `List<T>` passes wherever a `Stream<T>` is
asked for. A `Set<T>` is not a stream, because its enumeration order is not
part of the value. See [[collections]] for the collection family.

## Operations

| Operation | Input | Result |
| --- | --- | --- |
| `map(by)` | `F<T>`, any iterable | `F<U>`: a stream maps to a stream |
| `filter(by)` | `S`, any iterable | `S`, the input's own type |
| `drop(n)` | `S : Stream<T>` | `S`, without its first `n` values |
| `take(n)` | `Stream<T>` | `List<T>`: a bounded prefix is finite |
| `take(n)` | `S : Seq<T>` | `S` |
| `collect(n)` | `Stream<T>` | `Stream<List<T>>`: consecutive values in batches of `n` |
| `collect(n)` | `Seq<T>` | `List<List<T>>` |
| `emit` | `Stream<Seq<T>>` | `Stream<T>`: every member of every batch, in order |
| `emit` | `Seq<Seq<T>>` | `List<T>` |
| `flat_map(by)` | `Stream<T>`, `by : T -> Seq<U>` | `Stream<U>`, which is `map(by) \| emit` |
| `oks()`, `errs()` | `Stream<Result<T, E>>` | `Stream<T>`, `Stream<E>`: the payloads of one side, in order |
| `format(F)`, `text`, `json`, … | `Stream<T>` | `Stream<F>`: one piece of text per value, see [[hql/presentation]] |

`map`, `filter`, `take`, `drop`, `collect`, `emit`, `flat_map`, `oks` and `errs` consume one
value at a time, so a pipeline of them over a stream runs only as far as its
consumer asks. `collect(n)` holds at most `n` values: it passes on each batch
when the batch is full, and the last, shorter one when its input ends, so
`xs | collect(n) | emit` is `xs`. `n` is at least `1`. `emit` and `flat_map`
need sequences as batches: a set's members have no positions to emit them in.

`List(xs)`, or `xs | List`, materializes a `Seq<T>`. It refuses a stream that
may not end, naming `take(n)` and `collect(n)`, and a set, naming `sort`.

## A whole-input operation needs a finite input

`size`, `count`, `contains`, `sort`, `group`, `reduce`, `sum`, `min` and `max`
need the end of their input before they produce anything, so each requires a
`Collection<T>`. Over a stream that is not also a collection, each is a check
error naming `take(n)`:

```hql
naturals(1) | sum              // error: Stream<Int> may not end
naturals(1) | take(100) | sum  // Int
```

## An iterating function produces a stream

A user `fn` whose body is a block yields its values, and its declared result
says whether production ends:

```hql
fn naturals(from: Int) : Stream<Int> {
    yield from
    for n in naturals(from + 1) {
        yield n
    }
}

naturals(1)
| filter(n => n % 2 == 0)
| take(3)                      // [2, 4, 6]
```

- A block body holds `yield expr`, `for name in iterable { … }` and bindings
  `name = expr`, one per line. Each `yield` contributes one value, in the order
  it is reached.
- A block body is written only as the body of a user `fn`, whose result is
  `Seq<T>` or `Stream<T>`; every yielded value is a `T`. `yield` and `for`
  anywhere else are syntax errors.
- Calling an iterating function evaluates nothing. Its value is a restartable
  traversal: two uses of one binding see the same values.
- A `Seq<T>` result promises that production ends. A step that needs the whole
  input produces a finite traversal's values once and keeps them.

`cards` is such a sequence: the program module's binding
`cards = vault.cards`, a `Seq<Card>` in the order a sorted walk of the vault
reaches each card.
